Cream puffs with light cream
6
Choux buns filled with light cream: vanilla pastry cream lightened with whipped cream.

---
Choux pastry
water|250|ml
soft butter|100|g
fine salt|5|g
powdered sugar|5|g
wheat flour|150|g
eggs|225|g

Vanilla pastry cream
semi-skimmed milk|500|ml
egg yolks|80|g
powdered sugar|100|g
wheat flour|50|g
vanilla pod|0.5|pcs

Whipped cream
double cream|300|ml

Finish
egg yolk (glaze)|25|g
powdered sugar|30|g

---
Make the choux: preheat the oven to 180°C.
Put the water, butter, salt and sugar in a saucepan and bring to the boil.
Off the heat, add the flour all at once, mix, then return to medium heat to dry the dough.
When the dough forms a ball that pulls away from the sides, transfer to a bowl.
Add the eggs one by one, mixing vigorously, until you have a smooth dough.

Put the dough in a piping bag with a plain tip.
Pipe choux onto a baking-paper-lined tray.
Mix the yolk with a little water, brush with glaze and score lightly with a fork.
Bake about 25 min at 180°C, then cool on a rack.

Make the pastry cream: bring the milk and the split and scraped half vanilla pod to the boil.
Whisk the yolks with the sugar until pale, then add the flour.
Pour the boiling milk in while whisking, return to medium heat and cook 3 min stirring until thick.
Pour the cream onto cling film, cover with film and let cool.

Whip the cold cream to soft peaks.
Whisk the cooled pastry cream to loosen it, then gently fold in the whipped cream.
Put the light cream in a piping bag with a star tip.

Cut the choux in half.
Pipe a good dollop of light cream onto the base, replace the top.
Dust with powdered sugar before serving.

---
Scoring the choux with a fork helps prevent splitting during baking.
Do not open the oven door during baking or the choux may collapse.
Cool the pastry cream well before adding the whipped cream so it stays light.

---
